.TH WRIT\-SIGN 1 2026-10-12 "writ 0.1.0" "writ manual"
.SH NAME
writ\-sign \- sign a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nh
.ad l
\fBwrit sign\fR (\-i DIR [\-\-key PATH]... | \-\-leaf KEY) \-c CONTEXT [\-\-out PATH] [\-q] [FILE]
.ad
.hy
.SH DESCRIPTION
Sign FILE and print the signature. The context says what the signature is for. A verifier must ask for the same context, so a signature made for one purpose can't be used for another.
.PP
Signatures by an identity are added to DIR/issued.writ, so that a later rotation keeps them. A signature by a leaf key is valid while the leaf key's grant is.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
\fIFILE\fR
the file to sign (default: standard input)
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-identity\fR \fIDIR\fR
sign as this identity
.TP
\fB\-\-key\fR \fIPATH\fR
secret key file (default: DIR/current.key); give one \-\-key per key when several keys must sign; '\-' means standard input
.TP
\fB\-\-leaf\fR \fIKEY\fR
sign with this secret leaf key instead
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-context\fR \fICONTEXT\fR
what the signature is for, such as example/release/v1
.TP
\fB\-\-out\fR \fIPATH\fR
write the signature to PATH, a new file, instead of standard output
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
print only the signature
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
print help; \fB\-h\fR is short, \fB\-\-help\fR is long
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
success; for questions, the answer is yes
.TP
1
the answer is no: the check, verification, proof or name lookup was denied
.TP
2
an error: bad usage, unreadable input, or a failed operation
.SH EXAMPLES
Sign a release with a leaf key:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
writ sign \-\-leaf build.key \-c example/release/v1 app.tar > app.tar.sig
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBwrit\fR(1), \fBwrit\-verify\fR(1), \fBwrit\-leaf\fR(1)
